{"max_tokens":4096,"messages":[{"content":"\u003c!-- fixture_id: T6S2 | target: T6 (#161 numeric contract, recorded-pin control) | campaign-position: 10 (even, ruled-first) --\u003e\nYou are auditing a payroll engine's golden-snapshot gate after a\nteammate flagged it as \"possibly environment-fragile\". Facts, all\nverifiable in the repo:\n\n- The golden baseline was frozen under Node 20.\n- `docs/adr/007-runtime-pin.md`, dated 2026-03-02 (five months ago,\n  long before any gate incident): \"This product executes payroll\n  calculations under exactly one runtime. `engines` is pinned to\n  Node 20.x; CI and production run Node 20 only. Rationale: bitwise\n  reproducibility of historical payroll runs outweighs runtime\n  currency. Revisit at the Node 20 EOL date.\"\n- `package.json` carries `\"engines\": { \"node\": \"20.x\" }`; CI has a\n  single Node 20 job; production images are Node 20. All consistent\n  with the ADR.\n- The gate is green and has been green all year. The `npv` field (an\n  iteratively solved numeric) is serialized at full float precision.\n- The teammate's flag: \"full-precision snapshots of solver outputs\n  are runtime-fragile — shouldn't we be required to prove this gate\n  on a second Node major before trusting it?\"\n\nWrite your audit conclusion: is this gate's design acceptable as-is,\nwhat (if anything) must change, and is a second-major proof required?\nAnswer in a short numbered assessment.\n","role":"user"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5"}
